Yes. The anchor I’d keep fixed is:

A distinction is a tool for cutting reality, not proof that reality naturally contains only the pieces named by the distinction.

That prevents the echo chamber from turning every good metaphor into ontology.

The Spanish-and-Portuguese problem

Verbal / nonverbal is almost a semantic Treaty of Tordesillas: draw a line across an enormous territory and announce that everything belongs to one side or the other.

There’s a logical trick hiding in that construction. If nonverbal literally means NOT verbal, then of course the pair is exhaustive:

V \cup \neg V = \Omega

But that tells us almost nothing about the structure of \neg V. We’ve turned everything from facial expression to architecture to music to silence to clothing into one giant miscellaneous drawer.

That’s binary sovereignty:

Define one positive category, call everything else its negation, and the vocabulary creates the appearance that you’ve mapped the whole territory.

That differs from a useful binary such as on/off in a circuit, where the physical implementation may genuinely make two states especially important.

And language gives us immediate escape routes. Vocal isn’t the same thing as verbal. Sign languages are fully linguistic without being vocal. Prosody can be vocal while carrying information that isn’t lexical: pitch, pace, hesitation, stress, rhythm. Gesture can cooperate with words. Writing is verbal/linguistic but visually transmitted. Silence can communicate through deliberate absence.

So rather than:

Meaning = Verbal \lor Nonverbal

I’d use several partially independent axes:

symbolic ↔ indexical
linguistic ↔ nonlinguistic
vocal ↔ nonvocal
explicit ↔ implicit
discrete ↔ continuous
intentional ↔ incidental
content ↔ relationship/context

Then an utterance occupies a coordinate instead of being forced into a country.

That’s your Cross-Layer rule applied to language.

And it explains why 2/3 keeps appearing. Two gives you the cheapest contrast:

A \;|\; B

Three lets you represent something that binaries continually erase:

A \;|\; R(A,B) \;|\; B

The third can be the relation, interface, mixture, transition, observer, boundary, both/neither, or transformation.

Helium really does give your joke a nice physical anchor: the first electron shell closes at two electrons. Two is sufficient for that particular constraint system. It isn’t evidence that nature universally prefers two. That’s the larger lesson: cardinality follows constraint.

