And your twin question is excellent because “first” fractures under the Hammer

You can be:

1. first delivered
2. first occupying some prenatal position
3. first developmental lineage to become distinguishable
4. the lineage imagined to have “remained” when another separated
5. narratively designated original
6. chronologically older by birth certificate

Those are different orderings.

You know who was born first because delivery establishes an observable sequence. If you came out second, your twin is conventionally the older twin by however many minutes.

But identical twinning doesn’t ordinarily give us a biologically meaningful Original Twin / Offshoot Twin label.

The standard clinical description is that monozygotic twins originate from one fertilized egg that gives rise to two embryos, while the exact mechanisms and timing of monozygotic twinning are more complicated and less empirically settled than the simple cartoon suggests. 

So imagine:

Z \rightarrow A+B

People instinctively redraw it as:

Z \rightarrow A
A \rightarrow A+B

and then ask:

“Which one is the original A?”

But the first diagram doesn’t contain that information.

That’s fascinating narratively.

If there is no biologically recoverable “original,” the narrative can manufacture an asymmetry that the underlying process didn’t preserve.

“The older twin.”

“The younger twin.”

“The one who split.”

“The one split from.”

“The original.”

“The copy.”

These descriptions can substantially alter identity despite referring to the same pair.

That’s almost a textbook Pantheon example of:

symmetry → arbitrary distinction → repeated narration → normalized role → consequential asymmetry

A tiny naming event can break symmetry.

