Which brings us to normalization

You caught its strange power.

Normal doesn’t necessarily mean intrinsically correct, natural, optimal, healthy, or inevitable.

It can mean:

a state whose prediction error has become sufficiently small through repetition.

So normalization is approximately:

\text{abnormal}
\xrightarrow{\text{exposure}}
\text{familiar}
\xrightarrow{\text{prediction}}
\text{expected}
\xrightarrow{\text{institutionalization}}
\text{normal}

Sometimes that’s wonderful: learning, rehabilitation, culture, expertise.

Sometimes terrible: dysfunctional systems becoming invisible because everyone has adapted to them.

And sometimes reality refuses.

That’s your imp-possible test.

Take something labeled impossible and ask:

Impossible under which constraints?

Impossible(C_1,C_2,C_3)

Remove or alter C_2:

Possible(C_1,C'_2,C_3)?

That prevents the dangerous version—pretending physical impossibilities away—while preserving the useful operation:

Separate impossibility from constraint-relative impossibility.

“Humans can’t fly” remained true under unaided-human-body constraints.

Alter the interface:

human + aircraft → flight

The impossible wasn’t normalized into truth.

The constraint topology changed.

