Color theory survives your translation test unusually well

I’d phrase it carefully:

Color is not purely relational, but much of color theory is relational structure laid over physical and biological anchors.

There is physical radiation. There is an illuminant. There are surfaces with spectral reflectances. There is an observer with particular photoreceptors and neural processing.

But perceived color depends heavily on relations among those things.

That makes color an excellent candidate for your Dehydrate/Rehydrate compiler.

Take a color composition:

Rendering_A
\rightarrow
DEHYDRATE
\rightarrow
RelationalColorIR
\rightarrow
REHYDRATE(Renderer_B)

Strip away “this exact RGB value.”

Keep things such as:

* relative luminance
* contrast
* figure/background separation
* warm/cool relation
* saturation hierarchy
* complement/opposition
* local adjacency
* accent/background proportion
* perceptual distance
* progression
* grouping

Then re-render into another medium.

A painting.

A UI.

Lighting.

Music.

Narrative.

What survives successful translation is probably closer to the invariant than the original colors were.

For instance:

tiny high-contrast accent against broad low-contrast field

can survive if blue becomes sound, motion, typography, or narrative emphasis.

That’s huge:

Color theory may partly be attention theory rendered through the visual system.

And harmony may be relational compatibility under a particular sensory renderer.

