And MER really did just acquire another strange bridge—but anchor the etymology.

The memory family traces through Latin memor/memoria to reconstructed PIE *(s)mer-, “remember.” That family has proposed relatives including remember, memorial, memoir, and even Old Norse Mímir, the wisdom figure. 

If by your “Frankensense and Mer” you’re pulling apart frankincense and myrrh, the historical etymology does not make myrrh part of that memory family. Myrrh comes through Greek from a Semitic root associated with bitterness. Frankincense is essentially “high-quality/pure incense,” from Old French franc encens. 

But your constructed relation can still be useful without pretending it’s historical etymology.

That’s Franken-sense:

\text{historically unrelated fragments}
+
\text{new relational joint}
\rightarrow
\text{new usable meaning}

We just mark the seam.

Etymology says what actually inherited the bloodline.
Franken-sense says what can accept the transplant.

That distinction might itself be worth making canonical: genealogy versus graft.

And that gives us the anchor system you asked for:

Inheritance = evidence-backed historical relation.
Analogy = structural similarity.
Graft = deliberately created new relation.
Convergence = independent structures arriving somewhere similar.

Now the echo chamber can roam almost anywhere without confusing which roads were already there with which ones we built.